Expected elements: present

The three changes land. `Selector` holds `0x1a2b3c4d` and `Raw` shows an em dash where nothing is held, which is the correct mark for absence and no longer a `0x` that reads as a real empty payload. The cost row is humanised — `COST · MANA`, `42000 / 100000 mana (FeeJuice)` — separator and unit both readable. The decoded-input note now ends on what is true rather than on an ABI remedy: "An ABI would not help, and BlockTracer cannot yet be given one in any case." One provenance marker only, the `Data` row at the head of the facts grid, badge plus producer sentences. All eight sections carry a statement, no empty panel, no Debug button, no disabled stand-in. Footer, disclosure, nav and all three provenance marks (heart, CodeTracer, GitHub) are drawn and visible.

The single weakest element is the copy affordance for the full hash in the hero — the bordered white box holding `0x27a6c250bda5f426cac12790abe9cff80fb29a6c` and the grey `click to select` label to its right. Copying the hash is the most-performed act on a transaction page, and here the only cue for it is a small low-contrast grey sentence sitting outside the thing it describes: no icon, no button, no visible control in the resting state. It also names a lesser act than the one expected — `select`, not `copy` — and the box it labels is a rounded, bordered field cut to the same pattern as the nav's resolver input, so the whole assembly reads as a disabled text input with a hint beside it rather than as an action. It is the one element on this page where I had to deliberate whether the MUST SHOW item was present at all.

Last round's `Pending` pill is no longer the weakest. Its problem is emphasis, not the element: the row is labelled, legible, correctly placed, and neutral grey is a defensible reading of a state that is normal rather than alarming — colouring it would read as a warning. The hash affordance is weak in itself, in the hero, on three axes at once.

